Hi, Janis. Sorry to say I can read your German,
mostly, but no longer write it at all (my last
German classes were in 1963), so this reply has to
be in English.

I've had pretty good success with a product called
ws_ftp95 from these people:

http://www.ipswitch.com/Products/WS_FTP/home/index.html

The above link seems to be their current freeware
version.

FTP has the advantage that it knows how to change
the end of line marks among Unix, Macintosh, and
MS-Windows on the fly as the file is transfered.

It also can handle immense file sizes without a
hiccough.

I'm pretty sure, besides firing off the FTP daemon
on a Unix family OS, you need also to create a
dedicated file transfer area for FTP to use, and
attach FTP to the appropriate file transfer port
number (which I don't know off the top of my head).
Probably the FTP man page will be sufficient
guidance for you.

There should, normally, be an instance of FTP
already bundled with your Linux distribution, so
with the above product for the MS-Windows side, you
should be good to go, but you'd need to run the
transfers from MS-Windows, the above may not supply
a background client FTP for you.

FTP is also nice because you can wrap it in shell
scripts to check the transfer for errors, in case
you want to run FTP as a background or cron()-fired
process, something I did ten years ago while
implementing a world-wide transfer of meteorological
data for the US Navy's weather forecasting folks.

